{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"### The strategy\n\nWe can first rewrite each rational term of $g$ as a negative power of $x$.\n\nThen, the derivatives of these terms can be found using **the power rule**:\n\n$\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^n)=n\\cdot x^{n-1}$\n\n(Remember that this applies even when $n$ is negative.)","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"### Rewriting rational terms as negative powers\n\n$\\begin{align}\ng(x)\u0026=\\dfrac{4}{x^4}+\\dfrac{1}{x}+2\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=4x^{-4}+x^{-1}+2\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"### Differentiating using the power rule\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\u0026\\phantom{=}g'(x)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}(4x^{-4}+x^{-1}+2)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=4\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^{-4})+\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^{-1})+\\dfrac{d}{dx}(2)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=4(-4x^{-5})+(-1)x^{-2}+0\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-16x^{-5}-1x^{-2}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-\\dfrac{16}{x^5}-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion, $g'(x)=-\\dfrac{16}{x^5}-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"Let $g(x)=\\dfrac{4}{x^4}+\\dfrac{1}{x}+2$.\n\n$g'(x)=$ [[☃ expression 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"expression 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answerForms":[{"considered":"correct","form":false,"key":0,"simplify":false,"value":"-\\frac{16}{x^5}-\\frac{1}{x^2}"}],"buttonSets":["basic","prealgebra"],"functions":["f","g","h"],"times":false},"static":false,"type":"expression","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
